.TH SALVAGE 1 "2026-07-26" "salvage 1.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
salvage \- list files whose content is not present in a reference tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B salvage
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.I TARGET
.B \-r
.I REFERENCE
[\fB\-r\fR \fIREFERENCE\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B salvage
answers one question: if you delete
.IR TARGET ,
what do you lose?
.PP
Every file under
.I TARGET
is checked for matching content in one or more
.I REFERENCE
trees. Matching is by checksum, so a file that was renamed, moved, or
reorganised still counts as present. Names, paths and directory layout are
irrelevant on both sides.
.PP
.B salvage
only reports. It never copies, moves, or deletes anything. The list it writes
to standard output is intended to be handed to
.BR rsync (1)
if you want the files rescued.
.PP
.I TARGET
is the first positional argument. References are given with
.B \-r
and may be repeated; a file counts as matched if its content appears in
.I any
of them. Bare positional references are rejected, so a mistyped path cannot
silently become a reference and make coverage look better than it is.
.PP
.I TARGET
and
.I REFERENCE
trees may not overlap.
.BR rmlint (1)
will not call a file a duplicate of itself, so an overlapping run would report
every file as unmatched;
.B salvage
refuses to run rather than produce that answer.
.SH OUTPUT
.B salvage
writes to two streams, so one run serves both a reader and a pipeline.
.TP
.B stdout
The manifest: unmatched paths, one per line, relative to
.IR TARGET ,
sorted. Nothing else \(em no header, no counts. Relative paths are the default
because that is the form
.B rsync \-\-files\-from
requires.
.TP
.B stderr
The human report: a verdict line, coverage meters, a per\-directory rollup sorted
by size, the largest files at risk, and footnotes. A progress bar is shown while
scanning if stderr is a terminal.
.PP
The two coverage meters report the same scan by file count and by volume. They
diverge exactly when it matters \(em many small files copied while the large ones
were missed \(em so "92% of files but 20% of bytes" is a normal and important
reading.
.PP
When stderr is a terminal and colour is enabled, the report additionally draws
coverage bars and marks each path as an OSC 8 hyperlink, which most modern
terminals render as clickable. Redirecting stderr, or passing
.BR \-\-no\-color ,
yields the same report as plain text. stdout is identical either way.
.PP
The
.B largest at risk
list appears only when the unmatched list is too long to read in full; it is
usually a handful of files accounting for most of the total.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reference " " \fIDIR\fR
Reference tree to search. Repeatable. At least one is required.
.TP
.B \-\-absolute
Emit absolute paths instead of paths relative to
.IR TARGET .
Note that these are resolved paths, so symlinked parents appear in their
canonical form.
.TP
.B \-\-print0
Delimit stdout with NUL instead of newline. Use this for
.B xargs \-0
and whenever a filename may contain a newline.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Print the full file list on stderr regardless of its length. Without it, lists
longer than 50 entries are summarised on stderr; stdout is always complete.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-rollup
Omit the per\-directory summary.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-paranoid
Compare byte for byte instead of hashing with blake2b. Uses noticeably more
memory. See
.B HASHING
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " GLOB"
Exclude matching files. Repeatable. A glob containing no
.B /
is matched against basenames at any depth; otherwise it is matched against the
path relative to
.IR TARGET .
.B *
and
.B ?
do not cross
.BR / ;
.B **
does.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-default\-excludes
Do not exclude OS metadata. See
.B WHAT IS COMPARED
below.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-hidden
Skip dotfiles and dot\-directories entirely.
.TP
.B \-\-suggest
For each unmatched file, show where it would belong in the reference. See
.B PLACEMENT
below.
.TP
.BI \-\-suggest\-json " PATH"
Write the same suggestions to
.I PATH
as JSON: destination, reason, confidence, and whether the destination is
already occupied or collides with another suggestion.
.TP
.BI \-\-plan " PATH"
Write a reviewable
.BR sh (1)
script that copies each unmatched file to its suggested destination.
.B salvage does not run it.
Read it, then run it yourself.
.TP
.BI \-\-json " PATH"
Write a structured report to
.IR PATH :
counts, bytes at risk, and every unmatched file with its size. Diff two of
these over time to see what became newly unbacked.
.TP
.BI \-\-save\-scan " PATH"
Save the raw
.BR rmlint (1)
JSON, which
.B rmlint \-\-replay
can re\-process in seconds. Useful after a multi\-hour scan.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Pass rmlint's own output through.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the progress bar and the report. stdout is unaffected.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable colour.
.B NO_COLOR
in the environment does the same.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the versions of salvage, rmlint and jq. Include this in bug reports;
behaviour differences almost always trace to rmlint.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file in
.I TARGET
has a content match.
.TP
.B 1
Some files have no match.
.TP
.B 2
Error: usage, missing dependency, bad input, an unreadable file, or interrupt.
.PP
An unreadable file is always
.BR 2 ,
never
.BR 0 .
A permissions error must not be able to masquerade as a clean bill of health.
.PP
Because
.B 1
is a normal outcome rather than a failure, a caller running under
.B set \-e
must write
.B salvage ... || true
or test the status explicitly. This follows
.BR diff (1)
and
.BR grep (1).
.SH WHAT IS COMPARED
.TP
.B Hidden files are compared.
.IR .env ", " .gitignore ", " .git/
and everything like them. They are your data.
.TP
.B Empty files and symlinks are not.
Neither carries content that can be lost. Both are counted in a footnote so
the omission is never silent.
.TP
.B OS bookkeeping is excluded by default.
.IR .DS_Store ", " ._* ", " .Spotlight\-V100 ", " .fseventsd ", " .Trashes ,
.IR .TemporaryItems ", " .DocumentRevisions\-V100 ", " Thumbs.db ,
.IR desktop.ini ", " $RECYCLE.BIN ", " "System Volume Information" ", " .Trash\-* .
This is regenerated automatically and would otherwise bury the files you care
about. The count is always reported.
.B \-\-no\-default\-excludes
turns it off.
.PP
The distinction is that hidden
.I user data
is included while filesystem
.I scratch space
is not.
.SH PLACEMENT
.B \-\-suggest
answers the follow-up question: if a file exists nowhere else, where in the
reference should it go?
.PP
The destination is
.I learned,
not guessed.
.BR rmlint (1)
pairs every matched file with its reference twin, so each match reveals where
that directory's contents already live in the reference. A directory whose
files were reorganised or renamed on the backup side is therefore followed
correctly, not mirrored.
.PP
Each suggestion carries the reason it was reached:
.TP
.B siblings n/m
.I n
of
.I m
matched files in the same directory landed in this reference directory. A
unanimous vote is full confidence.
.TP
.B inherited from DIR/
The file's own directory contains no matched file, so the mapping of the
nearest mapped ancestor was used and the remaining path appended. Confidence
is reduced.
.TP
.B mirrored
Nothing above the file matched anything, so there is no signal at all and the
target's layout is reproduced under the first reference. Confidence is zero.
.TP
.B same name already in the reference, with different content
This is not a new file but a changed one. Copying it would overwrite the
reference's copy, so it is reported and, in a
.BR \-\-plan ,
emitted commented out.
.PP
Two unmatched files resolving to one destination are reported as a collision
and likewise commented out. With several references, placement follows
whichever reference the matched siblings landed in.
.PP
A plan copies rather than moves, so rerunning it is harmless and the target is
left intact. Verify with a second
.B salvage
run \(em exit status
.B 0
means the copy achieved full coverage \(em before deleting anything.
.SH HASHING
The default is blake2b at 512 bits. A collision would produce a false
.IR match \(em
salvage reporting that a file is safely backed up when it is not \(em which is
the only error mode here that loses data. At 512 bits a silent disk read error
is many orders of magnitude more likely.
.B \-\-paranoid
performs true byte\-for\-byte comparison if you want it regardless. Whichever
ran is printed in the report header, so a saved report records how it reached
its verdict.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check one drive against two backups:
.PP
.RS
.EX
salvage /Volumes/Old \-r /Volumes/BackupA \-r /Volumes/BackupB
.EE
.RE
.PP
Produce a manifest and rescue the files with rsync:
.PP
.RS
.EX
salvage ./old\-drive \-r ./backup > manifest.txt
rsync \-av \-\-files\-from=manifest.txt ./old\-drive/ ./rescued/
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the exit status directly:
.PP
.RS
.EX
salvage ./old\-drive \-r ./backup >/dev/null && echo "fully covered"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ignore build output and logs:
.PP
.RS
.EX
salvage ./project \-r ./backup \-\-exclude 'node_modules/**' \-\-exclude '*.log'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Safe against any filename:
.PP
.RS
.EX
salvage ./old \-r ./backup \-\-print0 | xargs \-0 \-n1 ls \-l
.EE
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, colour is disabled.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.BR rmlint (1)
version 2.10 or later, and
.BR jq (1).
Install both with
.BR "brew install rmlint jq" .
.PP
salvage itself is a single self\-contained shell script targeting bash 3.2,
the version macOS ships, and runs on both BSD and GNU userlands.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rmlint (1),
.BR rsync (1),
.BR jq (1),
.BR diff (1)
.SH BUGS
A filename containing a newline cannot be expressed in the newline\-delimited
manifest on stdout, and cannot be passed to
.BR "rsync \-\-files\-from" .
salvage detects this case and warns; use
.B \-\-print0
for a manifest that can represent it.
